WEBVTT

NOTE week11-clip2-reverse
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.929 line:2 align:center
A single network sees the noisy
structure x t and the step number t,

2
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:08.501 line:2 align:center
and predicts the noise that was added.

3
00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:13.072 line:2 align:center
Predicting the noise and predicting
the clean structure are the same job:

4
00:00:13.072 --> 00:00:16.283 line:2 align:center
either one gives you the other.

5
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:21.786 line:2 align:center
One reverse step moves a little way
toward the predicted clean structure,

6
00:00:21.786 --> 00:00:25.833 line:2 align:center
then adds a little fresh noise.
Repeat from a thousand down to one.

7
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:30.550 line:2 align:center
The fresh noise is what
keeps the samples diverse.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.893 line:2 align:center
Run that step a thousand times

9
00:00:32.893 --> 00:00:37.466 line:2 align:center
and the Gaussian cloud tightens
into a backbone that could fold.

10
00:00:37.466 --> 00:00:41.467 line:2 align:center
The drawing is illustrative,
but the trajectory is real:

11
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:45.350 line:2 align:center
structure appears
gradually, not all at once.

12
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.838 line:2 align:center
Start from different noise and you get a
different structure. This is generation,

13
00:00:50.838 --> 00:00:57.083 line:2 align:center
not retrieval: none of these has to
appear anywhere in the training set.

14
00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:03.570 line:2 align:center
RFdiffusion fine-tunes the structure
predictor RoseTTAFold into its denoiser.

15
00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.000 line:2 align:center
It works on residue frames, a
C-alpha position plus an orientation,

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.350 line:2 align:center
predicts the clean structure at every
step and feeds it back as a template,

17
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:19.274 line:2 align:center
and runs about 200 steps. One
twist: for motif scaffolding,

18
00:01:19.274 --> 00:01:22.482 line:2 align:center
adding no fresh noise
on the way back worked

19
00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:27.683 line:2 align:center
better in 17 of the 23 solved problems.

20
00:01:27.883 --> 00:01:31.093 line:2 align:center
RFdiffusion makes a shape, not a protein.

21
00:01:31.093 --> 00:01:35.676 line:2 align:center
ProteinMPNN then designs
sequences for that backbone,

22
00:01:35.676 --> 00:01:41.168 line:2 align:center
typically about eight of them. Those
sequences are what you can actually order,

23
00:01:41.168 --> 00:01:43.750 line:2 align:center
express, and test.

24
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.898 line:2 align:center
AlphaFold 3 uses a
diffusion module as well,

25
00:01:47.898 --> 00:01:51.331 line:2 align:center
but it predicts raw atom
coordinates directly,

26
00:01:51.331 --> 00:01:55.172 line:2 align:center
with no amino acid frames
and no torsion angles.

27
00:01:55.172 --> 00:02:01.133 line:2 align:center
Dropping that protein-specific machinery
is what lets one model cover proteins,

28
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:04.480 line:2 align:center
nucleic acids, ligands, and ions.
